\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Remark}
\title{The continuum commutator}
\date{2026-11-13}

\p{The continuum commutator in \ref{fdip-00AP} is
#{\bigl[\tfrac{d}{dx},x\bigr]=1}.
Physics writes the same relation as #{[p,x]=-\ui\hbar} with
#{p=-\ui\hbar\,d/dx}. The position-momentum realization in
\citet{Section 8.1, pp. 207-208}{teschl2014quantum} is this
comparison with #{\hbar=1}. That factor makes differentiation of
one real coordinate Hermitian. Differentiation and multiplication
by that coordinate are the operators that carried the factor. The
line has no unit bivector of a plane and no central pseudoscalar
of square #{-1}. A root of unity of the clock and shift, including
a primitive fourth root of square #{-1}, is a scalar of the discrete
operators in \ref{fdip-00AP} and is not that pseudoscalar.}
